    {
      "id": "ar-s-08#1",
      "len": 204,
      "source": "T:$\\cos x$ on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ (at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$: $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} > \\frac12$); $\\sin x$ on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ (at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$: $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} > \\frac12$)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-s-02#1",
      "len": 142,
      "source": "T:On $(-1, 0)$ the parabola is on top (at $x = -\\tfrac12$: $-1.75 > -3.5$); on $(0, 1)$ the line is on top (at $x = \\tfrac12$: $-2.5 > -3.75$)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-007",
      "len": 136,
      "source": "T:When every horizontal segment runs between the same two curves, but vertical segments would need different formulas in different parts"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-008",
      "len": 128,
      "source": "T:Solve $f(x) = g(x)$; the smallest and largest solutions are the limits, and one integral always covers everything between them"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-s-10#1",
      "len": 126,
      "source": "T:$y = x^3$ on $(-1, 0)$ (at $-\\tfrac12$: $-\\tfrac18 > -\\tfrac12$); $y = x$ on $(0, 1)$ (at $\\tfrac12$: $\\tfrac12 > \\tfrac18$)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-009",
      "len": 124,
      "source": "T:Evaluate both functions at one point strictly between $a$ and $c$; the larger value marks the top curve on all of $(a, c)$"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-008",
      "len": 121,
      "source": "T:Solve $f(x) = g(x)$; the smallest and largest solutions are the limits, and any solutions between them are split points"
    },
    {
      "id": "ip-f-009",
      "len": 117,
      "source": "T:If the original integral reappears after integrating by parts, the choice of $u$ was wrong and you must start over."
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-005",
      "len": 113,
      "source": "T:It is a signed total: $x - x^3 < 0$ on $(-1, 0)$, so that piece cancels the piece on $(0, 1)$; split at $x = 0$"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-006",
      "len": 113,
      "source": "T:The integrand is bottom − top: at $x = 0$ the line ($2$) is above the parabola ($0$), so the area is $\\tfrac92$"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-038",
      "len": 112,
      "source": "T:In $x$: two integrals, since the parabola crosses the $x$-axis at $x = 2$ and the integrand changes sign there"
    },
    {
      "id": "ip-f-009",
      "len": 112,
      "source": "T:A non-constant factor of the integrand may be left out of both $u$ and $dv$ and multiplied back in at the end."
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-041",
      "len": 111,
      "source": "T:One integral, $\\int_{-1}^{1} (x^2 - x^4)\\,dx$, because $x^2 \\ge x^4$ on $[-1, 1]$; at $x = 0$ they only touch"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-s-03#3",
      "len": 111,
      "source": "T:Test $y = 0$: the line gives $x = 1$, the parabola $x = -3$; so $R(y) = y + 1$ and $L(y) = \\frac{y^2}{2} - 3$"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-044",
      "len": 108,
      "source": "T:$y = x^3 - 3x$ on $(-\\sqrt3, 0)$ and $y = x$ on $(0, \\sqrt3)$, since the cubic changes sign at $\\pm\\sqrt3$"
    }
  ]
